<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manco Inca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1515–1544</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Manco Inca Yupanqui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manco <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> installed as Inca ruler in 1533. Ill-treated by the Spanish, he rebelled in 1536 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> for months, and won a battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ollantaytambo">Ollantaytambo</a> in 1537. Failing to retake the city, he withdrew to Vilcabamba in the forested mountains, founding the Neo-Inca State. He was killed in 1544 by Spanish fugitives he had sheltered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-inca.jpg" alt="Manco Inca" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SAHUARAURA_p108_MANCO_INCA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
